Devario provider usage panel (Zed 1.26.2-4)

Only Zed is patched. Stock OpenCode plus @slkiser/opencode-quota supplies the
usage data through the plugin's public v3 JSON export. Zed reads that local file
directly: no OpenCode patch, private ACP extension, custom agent name, or agent
connection is needed for the usage panel.

Open the Agent Panel and select Provider usage. All, OpenCode and Zed filters
separate the accounts. Select an account to expand its independent allowances.
The active conversation's context appears separately when the agent reports it.
Account allowance is never calculated from conversation tokens.

Setup

1. Add the plugin using your normal OpenCode installation:

     opencode plugin add @slkiser/opencode-quota

   Restart OpenCode and use /quota_status to check setup. Use a plugin release
   supporting JSON export schema version 3 (the 6.x plugin supports this).

2. Enable Quota file (JSON) on the plugin's Plugin tab, or merge this setting
   into opencode-quota/settings.jsonc inside your OpenCode config directory
   (normally ~/.config/opencode/opencode-quota/settings.jsonc on Linux):

     { "system": { "exportFile": true } }

   The default export is ~/.cache/opencode/quota-export.json, or
   $XDG_CACHE_HOME/opencode/quota-export.json. OpenCode Quota must run and perform
   quota checks to update it. Zed only reads the export; it never triggers a
   provider check. An existing export remains readable when OpenCode closes.

3. Install the patched Zed package and open Provider usage. Your existing
   OpenCode ACP configuration can remain unchanged, including registry-managed
   OpenCode. No project or active conversation is required to read the export.

If you use the plugin's custom system.exportPath, set OPENCODE_QUOTA_EXPORT_PATH
to the same absolute file path in ZED'S launch environment. A leading ~/ is
expanded. This variable no longer belongs in an agent_servers env object;
Zed reads the file itself. Restart Zed after changing its launch environment.
Without an override, Zed and the plugin need the same user/XDG cache directory.
The reader does not inspect private plugin settings or credentials.

Codex / ChatGPT quota setup with slkiser

Use OpenCode's OpenAI provider login for the slkiser export read by this panel.
A separate Codex CLI installation is not required for this setup.

1. Install @slkiser/opencode-quota and enable Quota file (JSON) as described
   under Setup. Start OpenCode with your normal user account:

     opencode

2. Enter /connect, choose OpenAI, then select Codex browser (legacy) or
   Codex device code (legacy). Complete authorization with the ChatGPT account
   whose allowance you want to see. These are the login methods required by
   slkiser's current provider documentation, checked 2026-11-04.

   The plugin currently reports quota unavailable for the separate Sign in with
   ChatGPT login method and for API-key logins. A login that can make model
   requests does not necessarily grant access to the quota endpoint.

3. Run /quota_settings and ensure the OpenAI provider is enabled. Then run
   /quota_status and /quota. Confirm that OpenAI allowance rows appear before
   troubleshooting Zed. The provider may be labeled OpenAI rather than Codex.

4. With JSON export enabled, open the patched Zed's Agent Panel > Provider
   usage > OpenCode and expand the OpenAI account. Refresh after the plugin
   updates its export; Zed reads the file at most once per minute while visible.

If the legacy login choices are absent, inspect the authentication options in
your OpenCode version; these instructions cannot substitute another credential
type. If the plugin reports quota unavailable for Sign in with ChatGPT, repeat
/connect using a supported Codex login when available. An existing Codex CLI
login alone is not the documented setup for slkiser; the auth.json fallback
described below belongs to the alternative usage-limits plugin.

  https://github.com/slkiser/opencode-quota/blob/main/docs/readme/providers.md#openai-and-xai

QwenCloud Token Plan through Bailian (working account setup)

Install Bailian globally with npm (requires Node.js and npm):

  sudo npm install -g bailian-cli
  bl --version

The following commands were confirmed working by the user for their QwenCloud
account. Run these login and quota commands as your normal user, without sudo:

  bl auth login --console --console-site international
  bl auth status --output json
  bl usage token-plan --output json

Complete the international console authorization in the browser. Check that
auth status identifies the intended account and that usage token-plan returns
its quota. The international site flag is needed on login; the successful
sequence above does not require an explicit region flag.

To connect this quota to the panel:

1. Keep Bailian installed and ensure command -v bl succeeds in the environment
   used to launch OpenCode. Slkiser uses bl for subsequent quota refreshes.
2. Restart OpenCode and enable Alibaba Token Plan in /quota_settings > Providers.
   The plugin uses this provider label even for this QwenCloud account.
3. Run /quota_status and /quota to verify that the plugin reads the allowance.
4. Enable Quota file (JSON) as described under Setup, then open the patched Zed's
   Agent Panel > Provider usage > OpenCode and refresh after the export updates.

This uses slkiser's existing Alibaba Token Plan reader, which calls
bl usage token-plan --output json. No direct qwencloud adapter is needed for an
account whose quota is accessible through this working Bailian route. The CLI
commands are user-confirmed; check the plugin and panel separately using the
steps above. Other accounts may have different console access.

The earlier blanket claim that a QwenCloud account cannot use Bailian for quota
was incorrect. The separate qwencloud CLI setup below is optional for this route.

  https://github.com/slkiser/opencode-quota/blob/main/docs/readme/providers.md#alibaba-token-plan

QwenCloud CLI setup (optional, separate management client)

QwenCloud's qwencloud command is separate from Qwen Code's qwen command and
Bailian's bl command. Installing or logging into one does not configure the
others. The instructions below install and authenticate the QwenCloud CLI.
They do not connect qwencloud directly to @slkiser/opencode-quota. Use the
working Bailian route above to feed the panel when it exposes your quota.

1. From the root of devario-custom-packagbuilds, review and build/install the
   included QwenCloud CLI recipe with Shelly:

     shelly build --help
     cat devario-development/qwencloud-cli/PKGBUILD
     shelly build devario-development/qwencloud-cli/PKGBUILD --sync-deps --install

   Shelly manages the system installation and dependencies. The recipe packages
   the official @qwencloud/qwencloud-cli 1.5.0 npm release and verifies its source
   checksum. npm is used only inside the package build. The build needs network
   access for npm dependencies. Run Shelly as your normal user and follow its
   privilege prompts. Do not run the login commands below as root.

2. Check that the command is available:

     command -v qwencloud
     qwencloud version

3. Start interactive account login:

     qwencloud auth login

   Follow the browser authorization flow using the QwenCloud account that owns
   your subscription. For a terminal without a browser, initialize device login:

     qwencloud auth login --init-only --format json

   Open the returned verification URL and finish authorization, then run:

     qwencloud auth login --complete --timeout 180

4. Verify authentication and query the account:

     qwencloud auth status --format json
     qwencloud subscription status --plan token --format json
     qwencloud usage summary --format json

   auth status should report authenticated and server_verified as true. A model
   API key is not a replacement for this management login. Subscription status
   and usage are different results; do not assume a usage total is a remaining
   allowance. If a command fails, use qwencloud doctor --format json to diagnose
   it. Keep credentials and account output out of repository files.

Slkiser has no direct QwenCloud CLI adapter. Its custom providers accept HTTP
APIs or local estimates; they cannot execute qwencloud commands. Direct use of
this CLI would require a new adapter that validates quota results and publishes
them through the existing v3 export. That adapter is not included here and is
not required for the working Bailian route above. Do not rename or wrap
qwencloud as bl: their command syntax and response formats differ.

OpenCode terminal alternative (does not feed this Zed panel)

@mynameistito/opencode-usage-limits documents both Codex and QwenCloud support.
After installing and authenticating qwencloud above, install that plugin with
the OpenCode version packaged here (plugin add is global; no -g flag):

  opencode plugin add @mynameistito/opencode-usage-limits@latest

Merge into ~/.config/opencode/usage-limits.jsonc, preserving other settings:

  { "providers": { "codex": { "enabled": true }, "qwen": { "enabled": true } } }

Restart OpenCode and check its Usage Limits sidebar. Codex uses an existing
OpenCode OpenAI login or falls back to ~/.codex/auth.json. QwenCloud uses its
own CLI login. This alternative provides OpenCode TUI output, not the slkiser
JSON export consumed by this Zed patch. Installing it alongside slkiser does
not transfer its QwenCloud rows into slkiser's export.

References (checked 2026-11-04):

  https://docs.qwencloud.com/api-reference/preparation/cli
  https://github.com/slkiser/opencode-quota/blob/main/docs/readme/providers.md#custom-providers
  https://github.com/mynameistito/opencode-plugins/tree/main/packages/opencode-usage-limits

Alibaba and other providers

OpenCode Quota owns the provider readers, authentication, cache and refresh
schedules. Its readers include Alibaba Token Plan personal quotas and team-seat
credits, plus OpenAI, Claude, Copilot, OpenRouter, DeepSeek and others. Follow
its provider setup instructions; Alibaba still needs the Bailian CLI and the
appropriate console or team credentials. This patch does not install plugins
or provider CLIs, authenticate, or query providers on the plugin's behalf.

  https://github.com/slkiser/opencode-quota
  https://github.com/slkiser/opencode-quota/blob/main/docs/readme/providers.md#alibaba-token-plan
  https://github.com/slkiser/opencode-quota/blob/main/docs/readme/external-integration.md

The OpenCode list is shared local plugin state, not the current project's
credential inventory. It appears once even if several OpenCode agents are
configured. Account names are plugin display identities, not credential IDs;
Zed does not infer that they match a particular OpenCode key. Native Zed
accounts stay separate, even for the same provider.

Data handling

- Provider-reported, locally derived and estimated values remain distinguished.
- Partial results retain their available rows and show the plugin's errors.
- Text balances, budgets and status values are displayed as exported. Schema v3
  has no separate numeric used/limit/remaining amounts; Zed does not parse them
  out of formatted text or invent percentages for value rows.
- Percentage bars are bounded to 0..100. The original percentage remains in
  text, including negative values when a quota has been exceeded.
- Reset times and per-provider fetchedAt timestamps are preserved. Rereading
  an old export does not make it fresh. Values older than five minutes, or
  without a known observation time, are labeled Stale.
- The tab rereads at most once per minute while visible in the active panel.
  Refresh reads the latest export; it does not refresh the plugin's providers.
- A missing file shows setup guidance. Malformed/unsupported/oversized exports
  fail safely, with any retained last-known UI values marked Stale. Empty
  snapshots remove obsolete rows. Individual plugin failures show their status
  without borrowing prior values from another account.
- Reading happens on Zed's background executor. Only a regular file bounded
  to 2 MiB is read. No usage subprocess or credential read is needed. The reader
  writes no files; the plugin owns its persistent cache and export.

The export must be available on the machine running Zed. A remote OpenCode
agent's cache is not fetched through ACP; use a local plugin instance or arrange
for the export file to be available locally. Remote conversation context still
uses Zed's existing agent protocol as before.

Native Zed allowance readers remain available for compatible ChatGPT subscription
logins, OpenRouter, DeepSeek and Vercel AI Gateway. Other native providers report
unavailable until a native reader exists. Custom proxy credentials are not
forwarded to public provider billing endpoints.

Validation

check() runs the Rust allowance tests and the direct export reader's tests.
Synthetic fixtures cover account separation, provenance, formatted values,
partial/error states, old/missing timestamps, file updates, missing/empty/
malformed exports, size limits and export-path selection without live accounts.
Full release package builds and interactive GUI testing are separate from these
checks.
